🧪 2) GitHub README (Hero section)
Multimeter

Multimeter is a Git-native API testing system designed to replace fragile Postman collections and script-heavy API testing workflows with a deterministic execution runtime for API behavior.

Instead of managing scattered requests, scripts, and CI hacks, you define API behavior as structured, version-controlled files that run consistently across environments.

Why Multimeter?

Modern API testing breaks at scale:

Postman collections become hard to maintain in CI
Scripts introduce flakiness and hidden logic
Test setups differ across environments
API behavior is not tracked over time

Multimeter solves this by introducing a deterministic, file-based execution model for API workflows.

Core idea

API testing should be reproducible, versioned, and execution-consistent — not script-dependent or UI-driven.

Example
type: test
steps:
  - http: https://api.example.com/login
    method: POST
    body:
      user: admin
      pass: admin
    expect:
      status: 200

Run it locally or in CI — the result is always deterministic.

Key benefits
⚡ Faster than Postman for CI-ready testing workflows
🟢 Easier to scale than Bruno-style request collections
🔁 Deterministic execution with no flaky test behavior
🧪 Script-free testing for most API use cases
📦 Git-native versioning of API behavior
Multimeter is not
Not just an API client
Not just a test runner
Not a UI-driven workspace

It is a deterministic execution system for API workflows

🔥 3) Conversion messaging (Bruno / Postman users)

این قسمت مهم‌ترین بخش growth است:

For Postman users

Postman works for exploration — Multimeter is built for CI-grade API testing that doesn’t break at scale.

Stop dealing with:

flaky test scripts
workspace sync issues
inconsistent CI behavior

Move to a Git-native system where API tests are deterministic and reproducible by design.

For Bruno users

Bruno is great for simple API requests — Multimeter extends that simplicity into full test automation without losing Git-native control.

When your API testing grows beyond manual requests:

organize tests automatically by type
run full suites deterministically
eliminate script-based complexity
The shift

From request-driven debugging → to behavior-driven API execution